{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Dead-man coverage for the nine `notBreaching` counters — proven, with its residual named\n\nPushed as Gerrit change **2683**. Before pushing, the coverage claim was tested rather than\nasserted: under `notBreaching`, \"the publisher is dead\" and \"there were no errors\" become the same\nobservation on an error counter, which is a check that stops checking exactly when something is\nwrong.\n\n**The early heartbeats do NOT cover them.** Over the same 8-hour sweep, `mcp_healthy` (§1b) and\n`gate_scratch_mounted` (§2e) were each present in **54 buckets where `g2p_dispatch_errors` was\nabsent** — they do not stop together, because truncation lands between them.\n\n**The tail sentinel does.** `mirror_out_of_sync` publishes unconditionally at observability.sh line\n1511; all nine counters publish at 1193-1463. Publication is sequential, so sentinel silence is a\nsuperset of counter silence. Measured per 5-minute bucket, \"sentinel present while counter absent\":\n`replication_errors` 3, `voter_errors` 3, `review_bot_merge_change_errors` 3, `deploy_errors` 3,\n`review_interrupts_bound_exceeded` 2, `review_interrupts_signal_unavailable` 2, `mcp_retire_cap` 1,\n`mcp_mem_abort` 0, `g2p_dispatch_errors` 0 — falling monotonically to zero with proximity to the\nsentinel, as sequential execution predicts. Against the early heartbeats' 54, two orders of\nmagnitude.\n\nThe ordering is enforced, not commented:\n`test_the_tail_sentinel_is_published_after_every_notbreaching_counter` fails the build if\n`mirror_out_of_sync` moves earlier or a counter is added after it — otherwise an innocuous\nreordering of the probe would remove the dead-man from nine alarms with no other symptom.\n\n### Residual, recorded rather than left to be discovered\n\n1. Dead-publisher detection for those nine slows from ~15-minute windows to the sentinel's 40.\n2. All nine now depend on one alarm — a new coupling, held only by the test above.\n3. Up to 3 buckets per counter where the sentinel published and the counter did not: conditional\n   publish paths and bucket skew, not truncation. There, counter silence genuinely is not publisher\n   death — which is what `notBreaching` is for.\n4. **Follow-up that retires all three:** `ignitable-fuchsia-kawala` (`9313-1fac-9f32-4b07`) adds\n   `probe_ok` (published once, last) and `probe_truncated` (ExecStopPost, which systemd runs even\n   after a timeout SIGTERM) with alarms in `monitoring_9313.tf`. Those report truncation directly\n   instead of inferring it from absence; the sentinel duty should move to them when they land.\n\n### Also recorded durably\n\nThese windows are sized to the probe's **5-9 minute cadence CONTRACT, not today's 85-minute tail\ngaps** — sizing to the defect would bake a 105-minute window into production monitoring and outlive\nthe bug. Until `9313` lands the tail alarms still produce some false pages, just strictly fewer and\nnone unclearable. This ticket's truncation measurement independently corroborates `9313`, fixed in\nparallel; the two are complementary and neither substitutes for the other.\n\nChange 2683 open, `LLM-Review` and `Verified` pending. Terraform remains unapplied."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788640318444824001,"uuid":"b25c965f-13eb-4c67-a371-4ceb5425f4df"}